  "query_preview": "..\nThrough Saturday...\n\nUpper level troughing moving over the ridge will be the dominant\nfactor in the weather over the next couple of days. Today will \nsee 500-1000 J/kg of CAPE, 30-40kts of 0-6KM shear, and steep \nlapse rates create a favorable environment for thunderstorm \ndevelopment. The Storm Prediction Center has issued a Risk Level \n2 of 5 for severe storms. The main threat with these storms is the\npotential for wind gusts in excess of 70 mph and hail greater \nthan 1 inch. Thunderstorm chances (20-40%) increase along the \nwestern foothills around 12 PM and move east throughout the \nafternoon and evening.\n\nAreas west of Billings could see a period of elevated fire \nweather concerns on Sunday as pre-frontal conditions bring \nwesterly wind gusts of 15-30 mph, with humidity down near 20 \npercent. \n\nA low pressure system moving through southern Canada Monday will \nbring a cold front through the region decreasing temperatures from\nthe low to mid 90s F on Sunday to the 80s F Monday. Drier air is\nexpected to move in behind the cold front with minimum relative \nhumidity values expected to be in the teens to low twenties. \nBreezy winds of 20-30 kts are expected along the western foothills\nand from Northern Rosebud County southeast to Carter County. This\nwill lead to localized areas of elevated fire concern. Cooler\nconditions in the 80s F continue Tuesday. \n\nUpper level troughing begins to move off to the east Tuesday\nbringing high confidence in upper level ridging returning. This \nupper level ridging is expected to interact with an upper level \ntrough over the Pacific Northwest. At this time, models are in \nagreement that the upper level ridge will win keeping warmer \ntemperatures and drier conditions over our region, and cooler \nconditions to the west. Upper level high pressure centered over \nthe southwestern US is expected to bring in monsoonal high based \nmoisture bringing daily 10-40% chances for showers and \nthunderstorms Tuesday through the weekend. Temperatures will \ncontinue to be slightly above average in the high 80s and low 90s \nWednesday through Saturday. Torgerson",
